      "19240": {
        "comments": [
          {
            "count": 0,
            "is_markdown": false,
            "text": "unserialize() of an ascii-format stream does not notice end of input inside a string. The ascii reader fetches characters with fgetc() (file streams) or Rconn_fgetc() (text-mode connections), both of which return EOF without an error, so a declared string length larger than the remaining input fills the rest of the string with 0xff bytes and carries on. The binary and xdr readers already error in this situation.\n\nReproducible example:\n\nx <- strrep(\"a\", 100)\ns <- rawToChar(serialize(x, NULL, ascii = TRUE))\ntc <- textConnection(substr(s, 1, nchar(s) - 50))  # cut inside the string\nunserialize(tc)\n## observed: \"aaa...a\\n\\xff\\xff...\" (100 bytes, no error)\n## expected: an error\n\nAnalysis:\n\nInString() in src/main/serialize.c (r90731) loops for the declared length calling GetChar() and never checks for EOF, including after a backslash escape. Besides returning corrupt data, a short file declaring a huge string yields gigabytes of 0xff bytes that the invalid-string warning path in ReadChar() then re-encodes one iconv() call per byte; OSS-Fuzz found this as a timeout in load() on a 154-byte file (testcase 5785070217265152).\n\nProposed patch: attached as unserialize-ascii-eof.diff. It errors with \"read error\" when GetChar() returns EOF, and adds a regression test to tests/reg-tests-1e.R.\n\nThe patch is also submitted as https://github.com/r-devel/r-svn/pull/396, where CI results can be seen.",
